## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

❌ **Don't:**
1. Train from scratch immediately (fine-tune first!)
2. Overclaim results ("Revolutionary breakthrough!")
3. Compare only to weak baselines
4. Skip statistical significance tests
5. Forget to make code reproducible
6. Delay getting external feedback
7. Perfect the demo before validating the concept

✅ **Do:**
1. Start with quick validation experiment
2. Be honest about limitations
3. Compare to strong, recent baselines
4. Report mean ± std dev from multiple runs
5. Document everything (hyperparameters, seeds, etc.)
6. Share early and often for feedback
7. Prove PAKD works before investing in polish

---

## Frequently Asked Questions

**Q: What if PAKD fine-tuning doesn't show improvement?**
A: This is valuable info! Options:
1. Debug: Check if profile weights are applied correctly
2. Iterate: Try different weighting schemes
3. Pivot: Focus on ACC/SCPD as main contributions
4. Train from scratch: Maybe architecture limits adaptation

**Q: What if I don't have enough compute resources?**
A: You can still succeed:
- Kaggle: 30 hrs/week free GPU/TPU
- Colab: Free tier + Pro ($10/month)
- Fine-tuning: Much less compute than full training
- Smaller experiments: Use subset of data to iterate faster

**Q: How do I know if my results are good enough to publish?**
A: Check these criteria:
- [ ] >5% improvement on key metric
- [ ] Statistically significant (p < 0.05)
- [ ] Reproduces across 3+ runs
- [ ] Ablation shows each component helps
- [ ] At least as good as recent baseline

**Q: What if reviewers reject my paper?**
A: This is normal and expected:
- Acceptance rate ~20-25% for top conferences
- Incorporate feedback and resubmit elsewhere
- Workshop papers are easier to get accepted
- arXiv preprint still counts as your work
- Use feedback to improve methodology

**Q: Can I use this project for grad school applications?**
A: Absolutely! This demonstrates:
- Research ability (novel method + evaluation)
- Engineering skills (C++, Rust, Python, web dev)
- Communication (paper writing, documentation)
- Initiative (independent project)

Include in application:
- Link to demo
- Link to arXiv paper  
- Link to GitHub repo
- Mention in personal statement

